 <FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" ><B></b></font>
<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >Hire Samuel L. Jackson, Christopher Walken, Dennis Hopper, Brad Pitt, and Gary Oldman...and then give them crappy little parts in a movie staring Christian Slater and Patricia Arquette.<br>
<br>
I'm talking about True Romance.  This was Quentin Tarantino's first script, and I think it came out somewhere between Reservoir Dogs and Pulp Fiction.  It's got Tarantino language and Tarantino violence...but no Tarantino style.  It just sucked BIGTIME.<br>
<br>
The 5 a-list names I mentioned above are all completely wasted (a triple-entendre there).  First of all, none of them gets more than 5 minutes screen time, with Jackson's time clocking in at about 30 seconds.  3 of 'em get killed, 1 just disappears, and Pitt does a great job playing Kith...er...I mean a pothead.  But he's got like 3 lines.<br>
<br>
For chrissakes...Bronson friggin Pinchot got more screen time than any of them, and he's even playing about the only part that he can actually pull off:  a crappy actor.<br>
<br>
This is probably about the most inane movie I've seen in a long time.  The premise is tired (Slater and Arquette accidentally grab Walken's cocaine, and spend the rest of the movie trying to sell it to someone without getting killed by Walken's goons).  The ending is predictable (Slater and Arquette are about the only ones still alive, the drugs are gone, and the money is theirs).  The dialogue is lame (Slater kills Arquette's pimp and she says "That's so..."  and he says, "What?!"  And she says "That's so..."  and he says, "What?!!!"  And she says "That's so....romantic!")<br>
<br>
I guess probably what happened was that Tarantino suddenly became hot, so someone snatched up this script and rushed to put it out.  They got alot of cool names to stick on the project, but got them on cheap because of a) Tarantino's name on the script and b) the fact that they only had to pay them for 3 lines each.  But I think this movie is made more unwatchable by the fact that we've seen some of the other stuff he can do.  Some of the scenes are OK (Oldman as the pimp is still my favorite part).  Oh, and I almost forgot that Val Kilmer is in this movie but you never even see his face.  Not that I like Val Kilmer, but you'd think that for the part he plays, they'd hire Schecky Finklestein for all we care.<br>
<br>
Don't bother with this movie.<br>
<br>
On a side note, I'll be watching Fist of Legend tonight, and Legend of Drunken Master some time next week.  That should get this foul taste of sdd out of my mouth.<br>
<br>
__________________<br>
If I were creating a world, I wouldn't mess about with butterflies and daffodils! I would've started with lasers, eight o'clock, day one!</font></p>

